{
  // The test tree, type-checked. `server/tsconfig.json` deliberately leaves `tests` out — see the
  // comment there — so `bun run typecheck` reports CLEAN while real type errors sit in the suite:
  // broken `import type` paths that silently degrade a value to `any`, stubs missing methods the
  // interface requires, `number | undefined` passed where `number` is required. bun erases types
  // rather than checking them, so running the suite never catches any of it.
  //
  // This config EXTENDS the project's own, so the test tree is checked under exactly the options
  // `src` is checked under. Checking these files with an ad-hoc `tsc` invocation and hand-written
  // flags is unsound: it drags `src` in under the wrong options and emits a flood of errors that
  // are artefacts of the flags rather than defects.
  //
  // `include` is not merged by `extends`, it is replaced, so the inherited entries are repeated
  // here with `tests` added. The errors that already exist are frozen in
  // `server/tests/typecheck-baseline.txt`, and the gate fails only on an error that is not in it —
  // see `scripts/typecheck-tests.ts`.
  //
  // JSX AND THE APP'S OWN RESOLUTION, for the two `headless-components` suites. They render the
  // gallery components to prove what a headless turn can draw, so they import `app/src/**/*.tsx`
  // directly, and without `jsx` TypeScript refused each import with TS6142 — "resolved … but
  // `--jsx` is not set" — which meant the components were never checked at all and the fixtures
  // beside them degraded to `any` (four TS7031s and a TS7006 that were really the same hole).
  //
  // `jsx` alone is not enough: the gallery uses the app's `@/` alias and Vite's `import.meta.glob`,
  // and under this project neither resolves. So `paths` names the alias, the way `app/tsconfig.json`
  // does, and `vite-env.d.ts` is included for its `/// <reference types="vite/client" />`, the same
  // one line the app relies on. Together those are what make the components reachable; they do not
  // put `app/src` under this project wholesale — only what those two suites actually import.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["../app/src/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": [
    "src",
    "scripts",
    "../scripts/composio-smoke.ts",
    "tests",
    "../app/src/vite-env.d.ts"
  ],
  // ONE FILE IS EXCLUDED, deliberately, and it is the only one.
  //
  // `tests/recovery-knowledge-foundations.test.ts` is recovered evidence rather than a test this
  // repository writes: `recovery-plugin-assembly-foundations.test.ts` pins its bytes (95182, sha256
  // 8246be12…) and fails if they move. Unlike its siblings it has no entry in either inverse ledger
  // — `helpers/recovery-lint-source.ts` and `APPROVED_K106_PRIOR_TEST_INVERSES` — so there is no
  // sanctioned way to record a repair, and one is not something to invent while clearing type debt:
  // the ledger's whole point is that someone decided the change was allowed.
  //
  // Its single error was a `readonly` tuple compared with `toEqual`, which wants `string[]`. The
  // repair is a one-word edit, and that one word is what the byte pin exists to catch. Excluded
  // instead: checking a file that cannot be edited buys nothing, and its integrity is guarded one
  // file over, harder than this gate could.
  //
  // `node_modules` stays: setting `exclude` replaces the default rather than adding to it.
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "tests/recovery-knowledge-foundations.test.ts"]
}
